// =====================================================================
// Seed Company - Solution A: shift the date, keep it a date.
//
// Marcus's suggestion on Slack: add a derived date that is the close date
// plus 3 months, so the CALENDAR year of the shifted date equals the
// FISCAL year. Then `this year` on that field means the current fiscal
// year and the KPI cards "know the timeframe" without anyone typing
// 1 Oct 2025 - 30 Sep 2026 anywhere.
//
// It works, and this dashboard shows it working. It also shows the three
// things that go wrong, which is why the dashboard is laid out as
// answer-on-top / catch-underneath. Walk it top to bottom on the call.
//
// Self-contained: everything this page needs is in this folder
// (`seed_a_orders`, `seed_a`). Solution B is the same shift kept hidden
// behind text labels; C and D move it off the fact table entirely.
// =====================================================================
Dashboard seed_fy_solution_a {
  title: 'Seed Company - Solution A (Shifted Fiscal Date)'
  description: 'Fiscal year via a +3 month date shift. Shows what it solves and what it breaks.'

  block t_intro: TextBlock {
    content: @md
### Solution A - the fiscal calendar as a shifted date

`Fiscal Month Start` = close month **+ 3 months**. October 2025 becomes January
2026, so the shifted date's calendar year *is* the fiscal year.

The filter on the right is set to **`this year`** on that field. It is
resolving to 1 Oct 2025 - 30 Sep 2026 right now, and it will resolve to
FY2027 on 1 October without anyone editing this dashboard. That is the part
Seed asked for.

Now look at the bottom half.
    ;;
  }

  block f_fiscal_period: FilterBlock {
    label: 'Fiscal Period'
    type: 'field'
    source: FieldFilterSource {
      dataset: seed_a
      field: ref('seed_a_orders', 'fiscal_date_month_start')
    }
    default {
      operator: 'matches'
      value: 'this year'
    }
  }

  block f_close_date: FilterBlock {
    label: 'Close Date (real calendar date)'
    type: 'field'
    source: FieldFilterSource {
      dataset: seed_a
      field: ref('seed_a_orders', 'created_at')
    }
    default {
      operator: 'is'
      value: []
    }
  }

  block kpi_gmv: VizBlock {
    label: 'GMV (Fiscal Period)'
    viz: MetricKpi {
      dataset: seed_a
      value: VizFieldFull {
        ref: r(seed_a.gmv)
        label: 'GMV (Fiscal Period)'
        format {
          type: 'number'
          pattern: '#,##0'
        }
      }
      settings {
        alignment: 'left'
        metric_value {
          font_size: 30
        }
        label {
          font_size: 12
        }
      }
    }
  }

  block kpi_orders: VizBlock {
    label: 'Orders (Fiscal Period)'
    viz: MetricKpi {
      dataset: seed_a
      value: VizFieldFull {
        ref: r(seed_a.total_orders)
        label: 'Orders (Fiscal Period)'
        format {
          type: 'number'
          pattern: '#,##0'
        }
      }
      settings {
        alignment: 'left'
        metric_value {
          font_size: 30
        }
        label {
          font_size: 12
        }
      }
    }
  }

  block t_problem: TextBlock {
    content: @md
### What breaks

**1. The axis labels are wrong.** The chart on the left is grouped by quarter
of the *shifted* date. Holistics correctly says `Jan 2026` - because the
shifted date really is January 2026. But the deals in that bar closed in
**Oct-Dec 2025**. Every date label on this dashboard is three months late,
and nothing on screen says so.

**2. Two date filters, and only one of them is honest.** `Fiscal Period` takes
relative ranges like `this year` and gets the fiscal year right. But type an
absolute range into it - say `1 Oct 2025 to 31 Dec 2025` - and you get
**Jul-Sep 2025** instead, silently. So you need the second filter,
`Close Date`, for anything absolute. Two date filters on one dashboard, and
the user has to know which one to reach for.

**3. Day grain is lost.** The table on the right shows it: `+ 3 months` on a
day-grain date clamps at month ends. 29, 30 and 31 November all land on
28 February. You can never drill from a fiscal month back to the real days.

**4. You cannot pin a "prior FY" card beside a `this year` filter.** A metric
built on `Fiscal Year Offset` uses `max(fiscal_year_num)`, and `max()` is
evaluated *inside* the filter scope: the generated SQL puts the dashboard
filter in the first CTE and every later CTE, the max() included, reads from
it. With `this year` selected exactly one fiscal year is in scope, so
`offset == -1` matches nothing and the card comes back blank. The clean fix on
this shape is a dashboard UI setting - unmapping the filter from the block. That
is why the relative-year KPIs are on Solution B, where the year dropdown is left
open, and not here.

AQL does have a filter-bypass, `of_all()`, and on this shape it fills the card
rather than fixing it. Dropping the filter puts every fiscal year back in scope,
so `max()` has something to re-base against - but it re-bases onto the newest
fiscal year *present in the data*, not onto the fiscal year containing today.
Those are the same thing right up until the warehouse lags into a new fiscal year
or runs ahead of one, and then the card is quietly wrong rather than blank, which
is worse. The offset has to stop being an aggregate to be anchored to the
calendar. **Solution C** in this folder does that - the offset is a row-level
comparison against `CURRENT_DATE` on the date dimension - so its pinned cards
depend on the date, not on how far the load has got.

Solution A is fine for a KPI card with a relative range. It is not a fiscal
calendar.
    ;;
  }

  block v_by_quarter: VizBlock {
    label: 'GMV by Quarter of the Shifted Date'
    viz: ColumnChart {
      dataset: seed_a
      x_axis: VizFieldFull {
        ref: r(seed_a_orders.fiscal_date_month_start)
        transformation: 'datetrunc quarter'
        label: 'Quarter (of shifted date - reads 3 months late)'
        format {
          type: 'date'
          pattern: 'LLL yyyy'
        }
      }
      y_axis {
        series {
          field: VizFieldFull {
            ref: r(seed_a.gmv)
            label: 'GMV'
            format {
              type: 'number'
              pattern: 'inherited'
            }
          }
          settings {
            color_palette: 0
          }
        }
      }
      settings {
        row_limit: 5000
        x_axis_show_null_datetime: false
      }
    }
  }

  block v_daygrain: VizBlock {
    label: 'Day Grain: real close date vs shifted fiscal date'
    viz: DataTable {
      dataset: seed_a
      fields: [
        VizFieldFull {
          ref: r(seed_a_orders.created_at)
          transformation: 'datetrunc day'
          label: 'Real Close Date'
          format {
            type: 'date'
            pattern: 'dd LLL yyyy'
          }
        },
        VizFieldFull {
          ref: r(seed_a_orders.fiscal_date)
          transformation: 'datetrunc day'
          label: 'Fiscal Date (+3 months)'
          format {
            type: 'date'
            pattern: 'dd LLL yyyy'
          }
        },
        VizFieldFull {
          ref: r(seed_a.total_orders)
          label: 'Orders'
          format {
            type: 'number'
            pattern: '#,##0'
          }
        }
      ]
      settings {
        row_limit: 500
        show_row_number: false
        sorts: [
          SortSetting {
            field_index: 0
            direction: 'asc'
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  }

  view: CanvasLayout {
    label: 'View 1'
    width: 1460
    grid_size: 20
    block t_intro {
      position: pos(40, 20, 900, 220)
    }
    block f_fiscal_period {
      position: pos(960, 20, 440, 100)
    }
    block f_close_date {
      position: pos(960, 120, 440, 100)
    }
    block kpi_gmv {
      position: pos(20, 260, 700, 120)
    }
    block kpi_orders {
      position: pos(740, 260, 700, 120)
    }
    block t_problem {
      position: pos(20, 400, 1400, 380)
    }
    block v_by_quarter {
      position: pos(20, 800, 700, 400)
    }
    block v_daygrain {
      position: pos(740, 800, 700, 400)
    }
    mobile {
      mode: 'auto'
    }
  }
}